# Distribution ids. The packager reads them from here, and uploads to a site only
# when its id and its token are both present, so a missing one skips with a
# warning rather than failing the release.
#
# WoWInterface stays commented until that project exists. A placeholder id is
# worse than none: with a token set, the packager would attempt an upload to
# project 00000 and fail the release rather than skip it.
# ## X-WoWI-ID: 00000

# Load order: vendor, then namespace, then data, then UI, then the dispatcher that wires them together. Core is last because it registers events at load.
# Perf.lua loads after Scan.lua and Gear.lua so it can wrap their functions in
# place, and before Core.lua so /warband perf exists when the slash command
# is registered.
Vendor\LibDeflate.lua
Init.lua
Store.lua
Scan.lua
Instances.lua
Cooldowns.lua
Gear.lua
Perf.lua
Bundle.lua
Export.lua
Import.lua
Junk.lua
GearSet.lua
Roster.lua
UI.lua
Core.lua

# Bindings.xml is deliberately absent from this list. The client loads it by
# name from the addon folder; listing it here loads it a second time through the
# general UI XML parser, which does not know what a Binding is and says so three
# times on every login. tools/validate.mjs fails the build if it comes back.
